Radius of hydrogen like atom $(\mathrm{r})=\frac{52.9 \times \mathrm{n}^2}{2} \mathrm{pm}$ Given, $r=1.763 \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{~nm}\mathrm{n}=1\therefore \quad \frac{52.9 \times(1)^2}{\mathrm{Z}} \times 10^{-12} \mathrm{~m}=1.763 \times 10^{-2} \times 10^{-9} \mathrm{~m}$
or, $\mathrm{Z}=\frac{52.9 \times 10^{-12}}{1.763 \times 10^{-11}}\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \quad Z=29.89 \times 10^{-1} \\
& \mathrm{Z} \approx 3
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \mathrm{E}=-2.18 \times 10^{-18} \times \frac{\mathrm{Z}^2}{\mathrm{n}^2} \\
& =-2.18 \times 10^{-18} \times \frac{(3)^2}{(1)^2} \mathrm{~J} \\
& =-19.62 \times 10^{-18} \mathrm{~J} \\
& \mathbf{E}=-1.962 \times 10^{-17} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}$

Angular momentum of electrons in $\mathrm{H}$-atom in its ground state is given by

\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{\mathrm{nh}}{2 \pi}, \text { for ground state } \mathrm{n}=1 \\
& \Rightarrow \quad \frac{6.625 \times 10^{-34}}{2 \times 3.14} \mathrm{Js}=1.055 \times 10^{-34} \mathrm{Js} \\
& \Rightarrow 1055 \times 10^{-37} \mathrm{Js}
\end{aligned}

From Bohr's atomic model

\mathrm{mvr}=\frac{\mathrm{nh}}{2 \pi}

or, $\quad \mathrm{v}=\frac{\mathrm{nh}}{2 \pi \mathrm{mr}}$
when $\mathrm{n}=1, \mathrm{~h}=6.626 \times 10^{-31} \mathrm{~J}-\mathrm{s}, \mathrm{m}=9.11 \times 10^{-31} \mathrm{~kg}, \mathrm{r}=0.529 \times 10^{-10} \mathrm{~m}$
then $\mathrm{v}=2.18 \times 10^6 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$
\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \quad \frac{\text { speed of light }(\mathrm{c}) \text { in vacuum }}{\mathrm{v}} \\
& =\frac{3 \times 10^8 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}}{2.18 \times 10^6 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}}=\frac{137}{1}
\end{aligned}
